    "updated_at": "2021-11-26T18:30:43Z",
    "body": "The idea is that there are several ways that a user can do something that is perceived as a 'navigation' that will not actually reset any state in the document. Currently, I can think of:\r\n - initial page load\r\n - restoration from session history (bfcache)\r\n - [App history](https://wicg.github.io/app-history/) (SPA) navigation\r\n\r\nThere might be more, but these at least would increment this counter. The counter itself would be exposed in PerformanceEntry objects in the [Performance Timeline](https://w3c.github.io/performance-timeline/). This allows consumers of that timeline to segment performance events by user navigation, and to understand the performance of the page in that context.\r\n\r\nSee https://github.com/w3c/performance-timeline/issues/182 for more context, and https://github.com/w3c/performance-timeline/pull/192 for the actual proposed integration there.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
